# short-description: SD card image for the Raspberry Pi that can take in-place updates
# long-description: Moonforge's moonforge-image-base-raspberrypi.wks.in (vfat
# boot, rootfs, /data), with the root partition at a fixed size and a larger
# boot partition.
#
# An in-place update (tessaro-ctl update) rewrites the root filesystem into
# the partition that is already on the card, so a later image has to fit: the
# size is TESSARO_ROOTFS_SIZE, not whatever this build's rootfs needed. The
# boot partition goes from 100M to 512M because the kernel now carries the
# initramfs that applies updates (~51M together, uncompressed) and an update
# writes the new one beside the old before the rename, next to the firmware,
# u-boot and every overlay. 256M would fit that today with little to spare,
# and growing it later costs every card a reflash.
#
# Unlike the x86 layouts there are no PARTUUIDs to pin, and none are needed.
# An MBR PARTUUID is the disk signature, which this wic derives from
# SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH and has no option for, but nothing on this image names
# one: cmdline.txt says root=/dev/mmcblk0p2 and the fstab wic writes uses
# device nodes. The updater compares the partitions' geometry instead.
#
# Changing the partitions is a new disk layout: every card needs one full
# reflash before it can take updates again, and the updater says so.

part /boot --source bootimg-partition --ondisk mmcblk0 --fstype=vfat --label boot --active --align 4096 --size 512
part / --source rootfs --ondisk mmcblk0 --fstype=ext4 --label root --align 4096 --fixed-size ${TESSARO_ROOTFS_SIZE}
part /data --size "${IMAGE_DATA_MIN_SIZE}" --ondisk mmcblk0 --fstype=ext4 --label data --align 4096
